<p>If you have a toddler, or a parent who eats alone, please read this before dinner tonight. I'll show you the gap nobody warned me about, and what's in our kitchen drawer now for the moment the Heimlich isn't working. It takes five minutes.</p>
<p>It was a Tuesday. 5:52 p.m. I know the time because the oven timer had just gone off.</p>
<p>Owen was three. He sat in his booster seat with a little bowl of grapes. I had cut them in half. I thought that was enough.</p>
<p>His big sister was telling us about recess. I was pulling chicken out of the oven.</p>
<p>Then the kitchen went quiet.</p>
<p>Not loud. Quiet.</p>
<p>Here's what nobody tells you about a child who is really choking. There's no sound. No cough. No cry. Just a look.</p>
<p>I turned around. Owen was staring right at me with both hands at his throat. His mouth was open. Nothing was coming out.</p>
<p>I dropped the pan.</p>
<p>I took a CPR class five years ago. I passed it. In that moment, I could not remember one single step.</p>
<p>So I did what my hands remembered. I pulled him out of his seat, bent him over my arm and hit his back. Five times. Hard.</p>
<p>Nothing.</p>
<p>I spun him around, put my fist above his belly button and pulled in and up. Once. Twice. Three times.</p>
<p>Nothing.</p>
<p>His lips were changing color. My husband was on the phone with 911. I heard him say our address twice. I heard the operator say the ambulance was about nine minutes away.</p>
<p>Nine minutes.</p>
<p>I remember thinking: he doesn't have nine minutes.</p>
<p>My arms were shaking so hard I wasn't sure I was even doing it right. I tried again. On the fourth thrust, half a grape shot out onto the floor.</p>
<p>Then he screamed. It was the best sound I have ever heard.</p>
<blockquote class="pull"><p>“For about forty seconds, my son couldn't breathe — and I was running out of things to try.”</p></blockquote>
`;

